{"from": "site:antonia-fortress", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-22-24-barracks-scourging", "grade": "B", "note": "The Herodian citadel adjoining the Temple was the tribune's garrison headquarters in Jerusalem - the barracks into which Paul was brought for examination."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-238-247", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-22-24-barracks-scourging", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus, War 5.5.8: 'the tower of Antonia a guard to the temple; and in that tower were the guards' - a permanent garrison in the fortress whose commander Acts calls the commanding officer."}
{"from": "text:cicero-in-verres-2-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-22-25-citizen-scourging", "grade": "B", "note": "Cicero, In Verres 2.5.66: 'It is a crime to bind a Roman citizen; to scourge him is a wickedness; to put him to death is almost parricide' - the same citizen protection against scourging uncondemned that Acts 22:25 invokes (brief lead: reuse B)."}
{"from": "text:cicero-in-verres-2-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-22-26-29-tribune-afraid", "grade": "B", "note": "The same oration makes binding a Roman citizen itself a crime (2.5.66) - the legal exposure behind the tribune's fear because he had bound Paul."}
{"from": "text:cassius-dio-60-17-5-6", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-22-28-tribune-bought-citizenship", "grade": "B", "note": "Dio 60.17.5-6: under Claudius many 'bought it from Messalina and the imperial freedmen', at first 'only for large sums' - the practice of buying citizenship for a great price in exactly the tribune's period."}
